course: biology
lesson: 337
title: The park pond through the year: ducks, coots, swans, dragonflies and ice
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A park pond is a small world that changes with the seasons, and if you visit it every month you meet the same neighbours doing different things.
explain: In spring a mallard mother marches a wobbly line of ducklings across the path while a swan hisses at anyone near its nest. In summer a dragonfly zooms past, snatching flies with its legs held like a basket, and pond skaters run about on top of the water as if it were a trampoline.
explain: In winter the ducks stand on the ice on bare feet and do not freeze, and two black birds argue at the edge: a coot with a white beak and forehead, and a moorhen with a red and yellow beak.
explain: Cartoon ducks gobble whole loaves of bread and cartoon swans break people's arms. Real ducks are better off with peas and oats, and a swan just wants you to give it space.
fact: A coot is a black water bird with a white beak and a white forehead patch, and a moorhen is a black bird with a red and yellow beak; coots dive more and moorhens walk more on the banks.
fact: The saying «bald as a coot» comes from the coot's bare white forehead patch, which is not really bald.
fact: A coot has three toes forward and one back, each with flat flaps called lobes that spread when swimming and fold when walking; ducks have webbed feet, and moorhens have long toes with no flaps for walking on floating plants.
fact: Ducklings follow the first moving thing they see after hatching, usually their mother, and can swim and feed within a day; male mallards have green heads, and females are brown to hide on the nest.
fact: Bread fills ducks up without much goodness, and leftover bread makes the water dirty and attracts rats; peas, oats, sweetcorn and duck food are better, in small amounts.
fact: Mute swans guard their nests and young and can be fierce in spring; the story that a swan can break a man's arm is not proven, and giving swans space keeps both safe.
fact: In Britain the Crown can claim unmarked mute swans on open water, and every summer swans on the River Thames are counted in a ceremony called swan upping, mainly to check their health.
fact: A dragonfly spends a year or more underwater as a nymph before climbing out, and catches insects in the air with its legs held like a basket; some dragonflies catch almost every insect they chase.
fact: A dragonfly has four wings that can each move on their own, so it can hover, fly backwards and turn sharply; dragonfly-like insects have been around for over 300 million years.
fact: Pond skaters walk on water by spreading their weight on long legs with water-repelling hairs, the water's skin holds them up, and they feel the ripples of insects that fall in.
fact: Ducks' feet stay just above freezing on ice because warm blood flowing in heats the cold blood coming back.
fact: People should never walk on pond ice, because it can break suddenly; if a dog goes onto the ice, nobody should follow it, but call for help.
fact: In summer ducks moult all their flight feathers at once and cannot fly for a few weeks, drakes turn brown like females to hide, and they grow bright new feathers by autumn.
exercise: How can you tell a coot from a moorhen? | A coot has a white beak and forehead; a moorhen has a red and yellow beak | A coot has a red and yellow beak; a moorhen has a white beak and a white forehead | A coot is white; a moorhen is black | They look exactly the same
exercise: Which of the two dives more and which walks more on the banks? | Coots dive more; moorhens walk more on the banks | Moorhens dive more; coots walk more on the banks | Both only walk | Both only fly
exercise: Where does the saying «bald as a coot» come from? | The coot's bare white forehead patch | Coots lose all their feathers in winter | Coots are born with no feathers at all | Coots pull out each other's feathers
exercise: What kind of feet does a coot have? | Toes with flat flaps that spread to swim and fold to walk | Fully webbed feet like a duck's, which are no good for walking | Long toes with no flaps at all | Hooves like a small deer
exercise: How many of a coot's toes point forward on each foot? | Three | Only one | Only two | All four
exercise: Whom do ducklings follow after hatching? | The first moving thing they see, usually their mother | The biggest and strongest duck they can find on the pond | Whoever feeds them bread first | Nobody, they swim off alone
exercise: How soon after hatching can ducklings swim and feed? | Within a day | After a month | After a whole year | Only after their first moult
exercise: Why are female mallards brown? | To stay hidden on the nest | Because they are older than males | Because they are ill in spring | To look like moorhens on the bank
exercise: Which food is better for ducks than bread? | Peas, oats and sweetcorn | Salty crisps and biscuits | Chocolate and birthday cake | More white bread, but toasted
exercise: Why is it better to give ducks small amounts of food? | Leftovers make the water dirty and attract rats | Big amounts make ducks so heavy they cannot fly away from foxes | Ducks cannot swallow big pieces | Ducks only eat once a week
exercise: When are mute swans most fierce? | In spring, guarding nests and young | In winter, when the pond freezes over | At night, when they cannot see | Only when it rains
exercise: What is swan upping? | A yearly count of the swans on the River Thames | Lifting all the swans out of the river and keeping them in for winter | A race between swans on the river | A dance swans do in spring
exercise: How does a dragonfly catch insects? | In the air, with its legs held like a basket | By diving underwater and grabbing them from below | With a long sticky tongue | By spinning a web over the pond
exercise: Where does a young dragonfly live? | Underwater as a nymph, for a year or more | In the mud on the bank, out of the water, for two years | In a cocoon on a leaf | In a nest in the reeds
exercise: What do a dragonfly's four separate wings let it do? | Hover, fly backwards and turn sharply | Fly only straight ahead, but very fast | Swim deep underwater to chase small fish | Sing loudly like a grasshopper in summer
exercise: What holds a pond skater up on the water? | The water's skin, with its weight spread on long hairy legs | Tiny bubbles of air trapped under its feet that work like floats | Its wings flapping very fast | It is lighter than air
exercise: Why don't ducks' feet freeze on ice? | Warm blood going in heats the cold blood coming back | Their feet are filled with feathers | They stand on one foot at a time | Duck feet have no blood
exercise: Why do drakes look brown like females in summer? | They moult, cannot fly for weeks, and hide in drab feathers | They roll in the pond mud to keep cool, and it stains their feathers | They are ill from eating bread | They are dressing up for the winter
test: «the swans are the King's, so if I nick one it's treason lol» What is true? | The Crown can claim unmarked mute swans on open water | Every wild bird in Britain belongs to the King by law | The King himself feeds every single swan in the country | Swans belong to whichever person feeds them bread most often
test: «that swan's hissing, my mate says one can snap your arm, run!» What is right? | That story is not proven; give the swan space, especially in spring | Hissing just means the swan wants some bread, so go closer and feed it | Swans break arms every day | Swans are harmless and love hugs
test: In a cartoon ducks gobble a whole loaf of bread and grow fat and happy. What is right? | Bread fills them up without much goodness and dirties the water | Bread makes ducks grow fat, strong and able to fly further | Bread is the perfect duck food | Ducks cannot eat bread at all
test: «the dog ran onto the ice, i'm going after him» What is best? | Do not go on the ice; call for help | Run onto the ice fast before it cracks | Throw big stones to break up the ice | Crawl out quietly on your hands and knees
test: Why is pond ice dangerous even when it looks thick? | It can break suddenly in places you cannot see | Thick ice is always too slippery | Ice melts the moment you step on it | Ducks bite people on the ice
test: «dragonflies are just big flies, they can't hunt» What is right? | Dragonflies are expert hunters that catch almost every insect they chase | Dragonflies are too slow to catch flies, so they eat dead ones | Dragonflies eat only leaves | Dragonflies sting people for food
test: A girl sees ducks who cannot fly in July and thinks they are hurt. What is true? | Ducks moult all their flight feathers at once and regrow them | They are hurt, and every one of them needs to be taken to a vet | They have forgotten how to fly | They are waiting for winter to fly away
test: How could you show that water has a skin? | Lay a dry paperclip gently on the water and watch it float | Pour salt into a glass | Stir the water fast | Freeze the water in a tray
test: Which feet would be best for both swimming and walking? | Toes with lobes that spread to swim and fold to walk | Webbed feet with no separate toes at all, like a flipper | Hooves | Toes with sticky pads
test: Count them: a mallard and 6 ducklings cross the path and 2 swans glide past. How many legs and how many beaks? | 18 legs and 9 beaks | 9 legs and 9 beaks | 18 legs and 7 beaks | 36 legs and 9 beaks
test: Count them: 3 dragonflies zoom over the pond. How many wings are flapping? | 12 | 6 | 3 | 18
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between the coot and the moorhen? | The coot bears a white bill and frontal patch, whereas the moorhen bears a bill of red and yellow | The coot is white in plumage, whereas the moorhen is black | The moorhen possesses lobed toes, whereas the coot possesses webbed feet | The two birds are indistinguishable in all respects
exam: Identify the principal reason for which bread is regarded as an unsuitable food for ducks. | It offers little nourishment, and the residue pollutes the water | It expands within the stomach and renders the bird unable to float | It is entirely indigestible to all waterfowl | Its provision is prohibited by the Crown
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ceremony of swan upping. | It constitutes an annual count of swans upon the River Thames, conducted chiefly to examine their health | It constitutes a race between swans upon the River Thames | It involves the removal of all swans to captivity for the winter | It involves the marking of every swan in Britain with a crown
exam: Ascertain the conduct appropriate should a dog venture onto the ice of a frozen pond. | No person should follow the animal onto the ice, and assistance should be summoned | The owner should proceed rapidly onto the ice to recover the dog | Stones should be thrown in order to break the ice beneath the dog | The owner should crawl onto the ice in silence
